I"<p>Job-intelligence reports relate reviewed skill evidence to occupations and separately sourced market information. They support investigation and institutional decisions, not a guarantee of a vacancy match, salary or employment.</p>

<h2 id="job-coverage-and-refresh">Job coverage and refresh</h2>

<p>CertifyMe’s own job-farming engine covers 20 million live jobs from 50,000 companies worldwide, refreshed every two weeks. Before using the data, confirm geographic coverage, permitted use and the availability of fields such as salary.</p>

<h2 id="use-an-evidencemappingmarket-chain">Use an evidence–mapping–market chain</h2>

<p>Start with an assessed achievement, review the skill relationship, relate it to an occupation and then inspect the separate market source. <a href="/skills-taxonomy-mapping">Skills taxonomy mapping</a> keeps academic evidence distinct from occupational vocabulary.</p>

<h2 id="keep-learner-records-attributable">Keep learner records attributable</h2>

<p>A <a href="/comprehensive-learner-record">Comprehensive Learner Record</a> connects achievements with provenance. It does not independently validate the original assessment or turn a market signal into a learner capability.</p>

<h2 id="interpret-the-report-rather-than-promising-a-result">Interpret the report rather than promising a result</h2>

<p>See how university teams use <a href="/workforce-intelligence">workforce intelligence</a> to investigate occupations and employer demand. Check source dates, geographic coverage, duplicate handling and the meaning of rankings before using a report in curriculum or career decisions.</p>

<h2 id="review-programme-relevance">Review programme relevance</h2>

<p>An academic team could compare a reviewed project-related skill with occupational descriptions and recent employer requirements. The result may suggest an assessment or curriculum question. It does not prove that the learner is ready for every matching role or that a particular vacancy remains open.</p>

<h2 id="design-the-institutional-workflow">Design the institutional workflow</h2>

<p>Within the <a href="/platform-overview">credential lifecycle</a>, assign academic approval, data review and learner-support responsibilities. Career services can help investigate gaps and suitable next steps; programme leaders should not infer placement from report views.</p>

<h2 id="what-evidence-can-support-a-claim">What evidence can support a claim?</h2>

<p>Read the <a href="/case-studies.html">customer case studies</a> for adoption and career-service engagement examples. These measures are distinct from hiring outcomes. Confirm data permissions and contract scope for your institution’s intended use.</p>

<h2 id="plan-your-next-step">Plan your next step</h2>

<p>Discuss the actual award, evidence and implementation requirements with your institution. <a href="https://info.certifyme.online/request-demo">Request an institutional demo</a>.</p>

<h2 id="the-infrastructure-connecting-learning-evidence-to-job-intelligence">The infrastructure connecting learning evidence to job intelligence</h2>

<p>The report is one output of CertifyMe’s Job Engine, not an isolated career-readiness claim. Reviewed learning evidence supplies the skill context; structured job records supply the employer and occupation context. Keep those two sources distinct when academic and career-services teams interpret a report.</p>

